**Shadow stage (2026-11-10 to 11-17).** v7 scores every search request alongside v6, but v6 still serves every page. v7's top 10 is only logged next to v6's, so no user ever sees it. The stage runs for at least 7 days, including a full weekend, because weekend queries differ and v6 regressed on them in 2024. To pass, all four of these must hold every day in `ranker_shadow_daily`:

- p99 scoring latency at or under 45 ms;
- timeout and error rate at or under 0.1%;
- top-10 overlap with v6 between 55% and 85%;
- no feature null in more than 2% of requests.

The search lead and the on-call SRE then sign off. The overlap ceiling is the criterion to watch: above 85% doesn't count as a safe pass. It stops the rollout for a modelling review, because v7 would be too close to v6 for an A/B test to measure a difference.

**Live A/B stage (2026-11-24 to 12-08).** 10% of logged-in users get v7 and another 10% stay on v6 as a control. Users are assigned by hashed `user_id`, so each one keeps the same arm on every device, and logged-out traffic stays on v6. v7 passes if successful-search rate (`ssr_v2`, averaged per user) rises by at least 1.0% relative. That test is one-sided at alpha 0.05 and is read only once, on day 14. Three guardrails must also hold on day 14: zero-result rate, page latency and revenue per user.

The test can stop early only for harm. That means a guardrail breached on two consecutive days, a Sev-2 or worse incident caused by v7, or SSR down 2% or more on any day. The revenue guardrail is effectively untestable, because the test can only detect a drop of about 4%. Revenue is therefore monitored rather than gated, and Finance will run a separate revenue holdout after launch.

One mismatch in the document: the exit step names the Thursday ranking review, but the timeline dates the shadow review 2026-11-20, which is a Friday.

**Dual-write (phase 1).** Legacy `pg-orders-1` stays the source of truth for all reads and writes. Each write also lands in an `orders_outbox` row in the same transaction, and `outbox-relay` replays those rows to orders-v2 asynchronously. `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders overnight, and nothing reads from orders-v2, so errors there are invisible to customers. The phase exits when four conditions hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- the `order-diff` mismatch rate is under 0.01% for 3 consecutive days;
- relay lag p99 is under 2 seconds for those same 3 days;
- a one-time full-table checksum matches, which is the only check that covers every old order.

**Cutover (phase 2).** Reads move first: `orders.read_from_v2` ramps through 1%, 10%, 50% and 100%, one step a day, and then holds at 100% for a 48-hour soak, with writes still going to both stores. Then `orders.write_legacy` is turned off, orders-v2 becomes the only store, and legacy stays read-only for 30 days before it is decommissioned. Up to the write stop, rollback is just setting the read flag back to 0, and nothing is lost. After it, rollback is impossible without losing orders, because nothing syncs orders-v2 back to legacy. That is why the write stop needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director.

One caveat: the timeline has no slack. The ramp ends 2026-11-27, the soak ends 2026-11-29, and the write stop is set for 2026-11-30. If the ramp holds at any step for even a day, the write stop moves into the 2026-12-01 code freeze. Dual-write would then keep running at full cost until January.

**Dual-write (phase 1).** Legacy `pg-orders-1` stays the source of truth: every write lands there and every read comes from there. Each write also adds a row to `orders_outbox` in the same transaction, and `outbox-relay` applies those rows to orders-v2 asynchronously. Meanwhile `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders overnight, taking about 11 nights. Nothing reads orders-v2 yet, so its errors can't reach customers. `order-diff` compares the two stores hourly. Cutover can't start until four things hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- the mismatch rate stays under 0.01% for 3 consecutive days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds for those same 3 days;
- a one-time full-table checksum matches.

The checksum is the only check that covers every old order.

**Cutover (phase 2).** Reads move to orders-v2 first, via `orders.read_from_v2`, at 1%, 10%, 50% and 100%, one step a day. Writes still go to both stores during the ramp, and then reads sit at 100% for a 48-hour soak. Up to this point, rollback just means setting the flag back to 0, and nothing is lost because legacy still has every write. Turning off `orders.write_legacy` is the one-way step: nothing syncs from v2 back to legacy, so falling back afterward would lose every order written since. That's why the write stop needs the soak to finish plus sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director. Legacy then stays up read-only for 30 days before it's decommissioned.

The schedule has no slack. The read ramp and soak take six days after the 11-21 checksum, and the backfill finish is only an estimate ("about 2026-11-20"). If anything slips past 2026-11-30, the code freeze pushes the write stop to January, and dual-write keeps running at full cost until then.
